Found during k101/k106. In t/http.t a stop() called at the top level (outside a running loop) can run queued callbacks partway through the stop: the k94 DELETE makes Mojo::UserAgent run one loop tick when the loop is not running -- an order the daemon never sees. Assertions that depend on that order can pass or fail for test-only reasons. Audit (mcp-hub-test-writer): find top-level stop/start calls whose assertions depend on tick order and drive them from inside the loop instead.

Audited t/http.t: only an HTTP-transport stop with a session id (k94 DELETE) and a top-level refresh_p of a ready upstream run a loop tick outside the loop; start_p never does. 18 stop sites + 1 refresh moved into the loop via _stop/_in_loop helpers. No assertion claim was wrong; no code bug found. prove -lr green, http.t 5x EV + 5x Poll green.

t/http.t only; patch + sha1 in w-k114 scratchpad

